{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DnEe1AvwGxNNgKyohRGmfh","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Enemy sprite style\n2\t\n3\tThe one rule for every enemy battle sprite (the user, 2026-11-04, tightened\n4\t2026-11-05). The 26 sprites the user picked, on the style board, show the look.\n5\tWhere a pick differs from a limit written here, the written limit wins.\n6\t\n7\t## The style\n8\t\n9\tNES style at every size. Sprites are crisp, chunky and readable, with flat\n10\tcolours and a black outline, and every pixel looks placed on purpose. A big enemy\n11\tis big flat shapes and few lines, at the same 1 px pixels as the player. Plain\n12\tareas are welcome.\n13\t\n14\t## Size\n15\t\n16\tEnemies grow as their tier rises, and each tier starts just above the one before\n17\tit. Within a tier, a creature's size in its lore sets where it falls in the range,\n18\tand your brief gives the exact number. A creature that is very small or very large\n19\tin its lore can go up to 10% past its tier's range. The game draws every sprite at\n20\t2x. Never scale art: one art pixel stays one art pixel. Keep the frame snug to the\n21\tdrawing, because the hit circle comes from the frame's size.\n22\t\n23\t| Tier | Longer side |\n24\t|---|---|\n25\t| Starter | 20 px |\n26\t| Low | 21 to 31 px |\n27\t| Medium | 33 to 44 px |\n28\t| Upper | 45 to 56 px |\n29\t| Hard | 57 to 66 px |\n30\t| Severe | 67 to 80 px |\n31\t| Elite | 81 to 96 px |\n32\t| Boss 1 | 100 to 150 px |\n33\t| Boss 2 | 160 to 260 px |\n34\t| Boss 3 | 280 px |\n35\t\n36\t## Shape\n37\t\n38\t- **One creature, one strong silhouette.** It reads at a glance on black.\n39\t- **Exaggerate what makes it that creature.** Small sprites can be stubby and\n40\t  cute, and bigger ones can take on real proportions.\n41\t- **Clean runs and clear steps.** Avoid long smooth curves that look like vector\n42\t  art.\n43\t- **Every part is its own part.** Limbs, fins and tails are separate, and each can\n44\t  be traced from the body to its tip. Parts never merge into one shape.\n45\t- **Proportions make sense** for that creature. It looks like it could stand and\n46\t  move.\n47\t- **No motif is repeated identically.** Scales, plates and bricks differ in size,\n48\t  spacing and shape, and some are left out. A few well-placed marks beat a pattern\n49\t  covering the body. Mirroring a whole body is fine.\n50\t\n51\t## Colour and shade\n52\t\n53\t- Only colours from the game's palette, 4 to 5 plus black. Up to 8 only when your\n54\t  brief says the design needs it.\n55\t- Flat hard fills. No gradients, soft edges or see-through pixels. No dithering,\n56\t  hatching or speckle.\n57\t- Two flat tones per material: a base and one shadow. No highlights and no gradual\n58\t  shading.\n59\t- Light comes from the upper left, and shade follows the body.\n60\t- Tiny details are welcome when each one is a real feature, such as a tooth, a\n61\t  claw, a horn tip or a stripe.\n62\t\n63\t## Line\n64\t\n65\t- A black outline, 1 px wide, round the silhouette. Never doubled.\n66\t- Black lines inside separate the parts. Use few of them: a line is there because\n67\t  two parts meet, not to fill a surface. A small sprite is about 8 to 15% black\n68\t  inside the outline, and a big flat one has less.\n69\t\n70\t## The black arena\n71\t\n72\tThe battle draws on pure black, so the player never sees a black outline.\n73\t\n74\t- No part may be drawn only in black, because it vanishes.\n75\t- No body may be mostly close to black.\n76\t- The sprite must read on black. Black is what the player gets.\n77\t\n78\t## Eyes\n79\t\n80\tEach eye is one solid mark of 1 to 2 px: black on lit skin, or one accent colour\n81\ton dark hide. Never a ring, an iris with a pupil or a white glint. X eyes and skull\n82\tsockets are allowed as character features.\n83\t\n84\t## Poses\n85\t\n86\tWhen a brief asks for poses: 4 to 5 distinct poses, each held 260 ms, with big\n87\tsteps between them. Every view and pose has the same body proportions. No water\n88\tunder an enemy.\n89\t\n90\t## Keeping the design\n91\t\n92\tThe user likes every enemy's design. Keep the creature in the brief: the same\n93\tsignature features, main colours and mood. Change how it is drawn, not what it is.\n94\t\n95\t## Done\n96\t\n97\tA sprite is done when the checker shows no failures and the user has approved it.\n98\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"22622122-a5da-459f-8fb1-37b57835c7bb","uuid":"2b0d8b02-a150-4cb5-a97a-1fa5b9df2c2f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T02:49:49.092Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/Users/13178/OneDrive - UWM/Desktop/projects/C-game/art/enemies/nes/93_chipique/RULE.md","content":"# Enemy sprite style\n\nThe one rule for every enemy battle sprite (the user, 2026-11-04, tightened\n2026-11-05). The 26 sprites the user picked, on the style board, show the look.\nWhere a pick differs from a limit written here, the written limit wins.\n\n## The style\n\nNES style at every size. Sprites are crisp, chunky and readable, with flat\ncolours and a black outline, and every pixel looks placed on purpose. A big enemy\nis big flat shapes and few lines, at the same 1 px pixels as the player. Plain\nareas are welcome.\n\n## Size\n\nEnemies grow as their tier rises, and each tier starts just above the one before\nit. Within a tier, a creature's size in its lore sets where it falls in the range,\nand your brief gives the exact number. A creature that is very small or very large\nin its lore can go up to 10% past its tier's range. The game draws every sprite at\n2x. Never scale art: one art pixel stays one art pixel. Keep the frame snug to the\ndrawing, because the hit circle comes from the frame's size.\n\n| Tier | Longer side |\n|---|---|\n| Starter | 20 px |\n| Low | 21 to 31 px |\n| Medium | 33 to 44 px |\n| Upper | 45 to 56 px |\n| Hard | 57 to 66 px |\n| Severe | 67 to 80 px |\n| Elite | 81 to 96 px |\n| Boss 1 | 100 to 150 px |\n| Boss 2 | 160 to 260 px |\n| Boss 3 | 280 px |\n\n## Shape\n\n- **One creature, one strong silhouette.** It reads at a glance on black.\n- **Exaggerate what makes it that creature.** Small sprites can be stubby and\n  cute, and bigger ones can take on real proportions.\n- **Clean runs and clear steps.** Avoid long smooth curves that look like vector\n  art.\n- **Every part is its own part.** Limbs, fins and tails are separate, and each can\n  be traced from the body to its tip. Parts never merge into one shape.\n- **Proportions make sense** for that creature. It looks like it could stand and\n  move.\n- **No motif is repeated identically.** Scales, plates and bricks differ in size,\n  spacing and shape, and some are left out. A few well-placed marks beat a pattern\n  covering the body. Mirroring a whole body is fine.\n\n## Colour and shade\n\n- Only colours from the game's palette, 4 to 5 plus black. Up to 8 only when your\n  brief says the design needs it.\n- Flat hard fills. No gradients, soft edges or see-through pixels. No dithering,\n  hatching or speckle.\n- Two flat tones per material: a base and one shadow. No highlights and no gradual\n  shading.\n- Light comes from the upper left, and shade follows the body.\n- Tiny details are welcome when each one is a real feature, such as a tooth, a\n  claw, a horn tip or a stripe.\n\n## Line\n\n- A black outline, 1 px wide, round the silhouette. Never doubled.\n- Black lines inside separate the parts. Use few of them: a line is there because\n  two parts meet, not to fill a surface. A small sprite is about 8 to 15% black\n  inside the outline, and a big flat one has less.\n\n## The black arena\n\nThe battle draws on pure black, so the player never sees a black outline.\n\n- No part may be drawn only in black, because it vanishes.\n- No body may be mostly close to black.\n- The sprite must read on black. Black is what the player gets.\n\n## Eyes\n\nEach eye is one solid mark of 1 to 2 px: black on lit skin, or one accent colour\non dark hide. Never a ring, an iris with a pupil or a white glint. X eyes and skull\nsockets are allowed as character features.\n\n## Poses\n\nWhen a brief asks for poses: 4 to 5 distinct poses, each held 260 ms, with big\nsteps between them. Every view and pose has the same body proportions. No water\nunder an enemy.\n\n## Keeping the design\n\nThe user likes every enemy's design. Keep the creature in the brief: the same\nsignature features, main colours and mood. Change how it is drawn, not what it is.\n\n## Done\n\nA sprite is done when the checker shows no failures and the user has approved it.\n","numLines":98,"startLine":1,"totalLines":98}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DnEe1AvwGxNNgKyohRGmfh","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
